* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈɛʃəlɒn/|/ˈeɪ-/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈɛʃəˌlɑn/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|ech|e|lon|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[level#Noun|level]] or [[rank#Noun|rank]] in an [[organization]], [[profession]], or [[society]].
# {{lb|en|cycling}} A [[line#Noun|line]] of [[rider]]s [[seek]]ing [[maximum]] [[drafting#Noun|drafting]] in a [[crosswind]], resulting in a [[diagonal]] line across the [[road]].
#* {{quote-journal|journal=Journal of Health, Physical Education, Recreation|year=1972|passage=In an '''echelon''', in which several cyclists are sitting in on one another, each rider takes his turn of about 200 meters at the front before dropping to the rear.}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Bicycling magazine's complete guide to riding and racing techniques|author=Fred Matheny|year=1989|passage=Cyclists in an '''echelon''' take up more room in the traffic lane than they do when riding single or double file.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} A [[formation]] of [[troops]], [[ship#Noun|ships]], etc., in diagonal [[parallel]] [[row#Noun|rows]]. {{defdate|from late 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-magazine|magazine=The United Service Magazine|page=183|date=October 1833|passage=The troops selected by his Royal Highness for this daring exploit, consisted of the war battalions of the 3d, 6th, and 7th regiments of the Royal Guard, forming the first '''echelon''', {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=The order in '''echelons''' is favorable for attack, because it readily conforms to the nature of the ground, and does not necessitate engaging more than a part of the forces; it is adopted for the purpose of attacking a particular point of the enemy's line.|year=1863|author=George Washington Cullum|title=Elements of Military Art and History: Comprising the History and Tactics of the Separate Arms; the Combination of the Arms; and the Minor Operations of War}}

